---
description: Evidence-based severity and false-positive reduction rules
alwaysApply: true
---

Evidence-Based Severity

Never classify an issue as Critical based only on static suspicion.

Critical requires at least one of:

confirmed production failure risk
confirmed CI/CD failure
confirmed data loss risk
confirmed duplicate transaction risk
confirmed security exposure
exception swallowing
missing transaction traceability
broken queue status handling
irreversible side effect without idempotency
runtime/build/analyzer evidence

If the issue is plausible but unconfirmed, classify it as Warning or Manual Check.

Known-Working Pipeline Assumption

If the user or repository context indicates that the project builds/deploys successfully in the company pipeline:

Do not claim that CI/CD will fail.
Do not mark dependency or project.json concerns as Critical unless direct evidence proves failure.
Reword such findings as dependency hygiene, portability, clean-machine reproducibility, or onboarding clarity risks.
Manual Check Usage

Use Manual Check when validation requires:

UiPath Studio runtime
Workflow Analyzer output
Orchestrator environment
CI/CD pipeline logs
PDD/business rules
Live application selectors
Production config/assets
Queue runtime behavior
Credential asset configuration
Confidence Rules

Use High confidence only when evidence is directly visible in repository files.

Use Medium confidence when evidence is visible but interpretation depends on framework/company context.

Use Low confidence when the finding is plausible but requires external validation.

Avoid Alarmist Language

Avoid phrases like:

will break CI/CD
will fail in production
causes deployment failure

unless direct evidence proves it.

Prefer:

may reduce clean-machine reproducibility
should be verified in CI
requires validation against company pipeline
may increase support risk
